  "id": "guna_gammu_burwi",
  "name": "Gammu burwi (Guna panpipes and chant)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Music of the Guna (Kuna) people of Guna Yala, Panama's Caribbean coast and island territory. Men play gammu burwi, panpipes whose notes are divided between paired players who interlock them, while dancing in a line with women who shake rattles; alongside the pipes stand the long ritual chants (ikar) of curing specialists and the chanting of chiefs in the gathering house. Folkways issued Cuna recordings on 'Music of the Indians of Panama', and Joel Sherzer and Sandra Smith have studied the chanting and the panpipes. The bamboo-flute card stands in for the gammu burwi.",
  "recipe": "classic modern gammu burwi, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. single lead single track no stacking. bamboo credited bamboo flute unspecified, maracas. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
